<h3>11. Build your own skills</h3>
<p>A skill is a <strong>reusable prompt file</strong> living under <code>.claude/skills/</code>. Define a long instruction once instead of pasting it every time, and Claude pulls it out when a request matches its description — or you invoke it directly with a slash. Anything you keep asking for over and over is a signal to make a skill. Making one is simple: say <strong>"turn what we just did into a skill"</strong> and Claude writes it. Devices like this, built for predictable results, are collectively called a harness — and skills are the textbook example.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">This wiki's <a href="#!skl-what">skill course</a> covers the topic in 12 parts, from principles to security. Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Skills</a></p>

<h3>12. Use a cheap model for subagents</h3>
<p>Searching and fetching text does not need a frontier model — using one is waste. When delegating, just say <strong>"run the subagents on Haiku,"</strong> or pin it with the <code>model: haiku</code> field in a custom subagent definition. Splitting the work — fast cheap models for collection, expensive ones for synthesis and design judgment — saves tokens in a big way.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Per-model pricing: <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official pricing docs</a> — as of this writing, Haiku 4.5 runs $1 in / $5 out per million tokens, a fraction of the top models.</p>

<h3>13. Update CLAUDE.md when a session ends — capped at 150–200 lines</h3>
<p>If a new convention or change came out of this session, put it in CLAUDE.md. Otherwise the Claude in your next session knows nothing about it. But CLAUDE.md is <strong>read unconditionally at every session start</strong>, so the longer it grows, the more startup context it eats — when you ask for the update, add "keep it under 150–200 lines" and Claude compresses accordingly. The official docs give the same advice: if you cannot skim it between meetings, it is too long.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Memory — CLAUDE.md</a></p>

<h3>14. Use CLAUDE.md as a router</h3>
<p>This resolves the tension between the cap in tip 13 and wanting to hand over lots of information. Instead of putting content into CLAUDE.md, put in <strong>a pointer: "if you need X, read this file."</strong> For example: "for the code structure, read <code>docs/architecture.md</code>." Claude goes to that file only when the need arises — full context delivered, standing cost saved. This too you can delegate: "add this as a router entry to CLAUDE.md."</p>

<h3>15. When something looks wrong, interrupt — don't wait</h3>
<p>The moment you see the direction is off, <strong>stop it</strong> (Esc). Then ask "my intent was X — why did you do it that way?" and Claude explains its reasoning; correct the course and let it continue. Waiting until the end wastes time and tokens, and worse, <strong>a wrong result sitting in the context contaminates every judgment after it</strong>. There is no reason to fear the stop button.</p>

<h3>16. Push back — and when it lands, record it</h3>
<p>First result disappointing, but you can't articulate the fix? Push back even abstractly — "make it more refined," "raise the completeness," "try a completely different angle." Claude now knows the first attempt missed, so the second try lands far more often. Most people try once and give up; push two or three times and you may be surprised. And <strong>the moment you get what you wanted is the moment to record it</strong> — "this is great, turn it into a skill" or "reflect this in CLAUDE.md." Skip the recording and you will walk the same road again next time.</p>

<h3>17. Rewind time with <code>/rewind</code></h3>
<p>When a mid-task result goes wrong, asking in prose to "please undo that" can introduce new errors during the undoing. Use <code>/rewind</code> instead — pick a point from the checkpoint list and <strong>every file edit after it rolls back wholesale</strong>. You can choose to restore the code, the conversation, or both.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/checkpointing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Checkpointing</a> — edits accumulate as checkpoints automatically during a session.</p>

<h3>18. Get notified with hooks</h3>
<p>A hook is a command that fires at specific moments in Claude Code's lifecycle — task finished, approval needed, and so on. Say "set up a hook that plays a sound whenever a session finishes its work" and Claude writes it into your settings file. With one session you won't need it; once you run several projects and sessions in parallel, a device that tells you <strong>what finished where</strong> becomes essential. The minimal version: set <code>preferredNotifChannel</code> to <code>terminal_bell</code>.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Automate actions with hooks</a> — see also this wiki's <a href="#!hooks">Hooks intro</a>.</p>

<h3>19. Claude has eyes — hand it images</h3>
<p>Error messages, a website you like, a UI screen — anything hard to describe in words, <strong>screenshot it and paste it in</strong>. Both the terminal and the desktop app accept dragged or pasted images, and Claude reads them. Throwing in a captured error screen beats transcribing the error text by hand, in both speed and accuracy.</p>

<h3>20. Turn screenshots into templates</h3>
<p>An application of tip 19. Capture a design you want as a reference — a card-news layout, a page with great color — hand it over and say explicitly: <strong>"make a template based on this image."</strong> The point is not copying it verbatim but extracting the color and layout structure into something reusable.</p>

